      "note": "We practiced a published RL method on math\nbefore applying it to RLMs. On the same 64\nquestions, correct answers rose from 20 to\n47. The chart keeps every check, including\nthe last dip.\n\nThis reproduces improvement at small\nscale, not the full benchmark result.\nTraining is stopped; checkpoints are\nsaved.\n\nNext: what made learning possible?"
    },
    {
      "idx": 1,
      "label": "2",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Walk through x = 4 versus x = 5. We\ncompare attempts on the SAME question. All\nwrong or all right gives this method no\nrelative reward signal. A mix gives it a\nuseful contrast.\n\nA curriculum could keep practice within\nreach, then raise the difficulty. Mixing\neasy and impossible questions is not\nenough.\n\nNext: can the model use its existing\nskills?"
    },
    {
      "idx": 2,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Point to the starting scores: 154 versus\n305 out of 500. Same model, different way\nof asking. A poor interface can hide\nability and make the training gain look\nlarger.\n\nKnowing Python is not the same as knowing\nour RLM tool interface or choosing useful\nsubtasks. Demonstrations may help; SFT is\nnot always needed.\n\nNext: what should we teach first?"
    },
    {
      "idx": 3,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Tell the invented example: Mira works at\nAster Lab; Aster Lab is in Oxford. The\nsecond lookup needs the employer's name.\nCalling a helper is not enough: it needs\nuseful context.\n\nTeach missing skills, then practice\nlinking them. More attempts may reveal\nrare successes, but can repeat the same\nmistake.\n\nThis sequence is a hypothesis to test."
    },
    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "These ingredients have precedents. DAPO\nkeeps informative reward groups. RLM work\nuses demonstrations. R1 adds supervised\npreparation; R1-Zero shows it is not\nalways required.\n\nNone establishes our proposed teaching\norder. Our question is which preparation\nhelps RLMs learn and combine skills on new\ntasks.\n\nNext: small experiments to distinguish\nideas."
    },
    {
      "idx": 5,
      "label": "6",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Start by locating the failure: tool use,\npassing context, choosing a plan, or using\nfeedback. Each suggests a different small\ntest.\n\nCompare staged versus shuffled examples;\nmore attempts at the same total cost;\nsuccess-only versus repair demonstrations.\nKeep helper models fixed and test new\ncombinations.\n\nClose: prepare learnable tasks, then test\nwhat helps."
